      "observedSourceAnswer": "Both CN575 and EN686 cross out xvi and specify that it fails only when f or g is the empty family.",
      "operatorSource": "EN293 printed 276 defines # by minimal hitting sets and ↓ by inclusion-minimal elements. EN290 printed 273 exercise203 defines ⊔ as {A ∪ B | A in f, B in g} without a disjointness condition, and ε is the singleton family containing the empty set.",
      "reasoning": "Each finite hitting set A for f ∪ g hits f and g separately, and contains respective inclusion-minimal hitting sets B and C. Their union B ∪ C is itself a hitting set for f ∪ g and lies in A. Conversely any B ∪ C with B in f^# and C in g^# hits f ∪ g. The inclusion-minimal elements of these two collections therefore coincide. The proof also covers empty collections: the empty family has the vacuous hitting set ∅, so ∅^#=ε; ε is the identity of ⊔. A family containing the empty set has no hitting set and yields the empty family on either side if it is an operand.",
